"OWR-2022-11 (https://publications.mfo.de/bitstream/handle/mfo/3944/OWR_2022_11.pdf?isAllowed=y&sequence=4)\nciteturn12search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.5 years ago; The first new group for which property (T) was proved using this new approach was Aut(F\\(_5\\)), the automorphism group of the free group on 5 generators [4]. The infinite case was settled subsequently by Kaluba, Kielak and Nowak [5] in the form of the following\n546                    Oberwolfach Report 11/2022\n\n# Property (T) for Aut(F_n)\n\n**Piotr W. Nowak**  \n(joint work with M. Kaluba and D. Kielak)\n\nProperty (T) was introduced by Kazhdan in 1966 and has become a fundamental rigidity property for groups. Only a few classes infinite groups are known to satisfy property (T), including most notably lattices in higher rank Lie groups, automorphism groups of thick buildings and certain random hyperbolic groups. We refer to [1] as the most comprehensive overview of property (T).\n\nRecently a new characterization of property (T) due to Ozawa [8] described property (T) for a finitely generated group G in terms of an algebraic spectral gap-type condition, expressed in the form of the equation\n\n(1)  \n\\[\n\\Delta^2 - \\lambda \\Delta = \\sum_{i=1}^{k} \\xi_i^* \\xi_i,\n\\]\n\nin the real group ring ℝG, where \\(\\Delta = 1 - |S|^{-1}\\sum_{s\\in S} s\\) is the Laplacian associated to a finite, symmetric generating set \\(S\\), \\(\\lambda > 0\\) and \\(\\xi_i \\in \\mathbb{R}G\\). This characterization allowed for a new strategy to be used to prove property (T) for infinite groups: solving (1) with the aid of positive definite programming. This method was first implemented by Netzer and Thom [7] to give a new proof of property (T) for the group SL\\(_3(\\mathbb{Z})\\) and to improve significantly the estimate of its Kazhdan constant. Similar result were later obtained for SL\\(_n(\\mathbb{Z})\\) by Fujiwara and Kabaya for \\(n = 3, 4\\) [2] and Kaluba and Nowak for \\(n = 3, 4, 5\\) [3].\n\nThe first new group for which property (T) was proved using this new approach was Aut(F\\(_5\\)), the automorphism group of the free group on 5 generators [4]. The infinite case was settled subsequently by Kaluba, Kielak and Nowak [5] in the form of the following\n\n**Theorem.** *The group Aut(F\\(_n\\)) has property (T) for \\(n \\ge 6\\).*\n\nAs numerical methods can only be applied to a single group at a time, a key ingredient of the proof of the above theorem is a technique of decomposing equation (1) in the group SAut(F\\(_n\\)), a subgroup of Aut(F\\(_n\\)) of index 2, into smaller pieces. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n$${\\text {Aut}}({\\mathbb {F}}_5)$$ Aut ( F 5 ) has property ( T ) (https://scispace.com/pdf/text-aut-mathbb-f-5-aut-f-5-has-property-t-52fyw5bgwg.pdf)\nciteturn12search13 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Aut(F₅) has property (T)\n\nMarek Kaluba¹,² • Piotr W. Nowak³ • Narutaka Ozawa⁴\nMathematische Annalen (2019) 375:1169–1191  \nhttps://doi.org/10.1007/s00208-019-01874-9  \nMathematische Annalen\n\nAut(F₅) has property (T)\n\nMarek Kaluba¹,² • Piotr W. Nowak³ • Narutaka Ozawa⁴\n\nReceived: 12 February 2019 / Revised: 11 July 2019 / Published online: 8 August 2019  \n© The Author(s) 2019\n\nAbstract  \nWe give a constructive, computer-assisted proof that Aut(F₅), the automorphism group of the free group on 5 generators, has Kazhdan’s property (T).\n\nBut they are useless. They can only give you answers.  \n— Pablo Picasso, speaking of computers\n\nContents\n\n1  Property (T), real algebraic geometry and semidefinite programming ............ 1171  \n   Complexity of the problem .................................................... 1174  \n2  Problem symmetrization ....................................................... 1174  \n   2.1  Invariant SDP problems .................................................. 1175  \n   2.2  Orbit symmetrization ............................................