mtp.cam.ac.uk/research/afha/anders/SCI_FINAL.pdf)\nciteturn12search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.0 years ago; *Group theory:* Aut(F\\_5) has property (T) - SCI classification:* ∈ Σ\\_1^A, ∉ Δ\\_1^G. ... Kaluba, P. Nowak and N.\nNevertheless, the problem is clearly ∈ Π\\_1^A, which is why it was possible to verify the counterexample.\n\n*Group theory:* Aut(F\\_5) has property (T) - SCI classification:* ∈ Σ\\_1^A, ∉ Δ\\_1^G. The fact that the automorphism group of the free group on five generators has Kazhdan’s property (T), was shown by M. Kaluba, P. Nowak and N. Ozawa [81]. The proof relies on a decision problem involving a minimizer of a semi-definite program (actually a root of a positive definite matrix that is a minimizer). The minimizer is computed using floating-point arithmetic. Hence, it is, at best (if one could do a backward error analysis), equivalent to solving the semi-definite program with inexact input. Computing minimizers to semi-definite programs with inexact yet arbitrary small precision is ∉ Δ\\_1^G [11]. Showing that the final decision problem used on [81] is ∈ Σ\\_1^A requires an argument, which we do not repeat here. However, it is similar to the argument above, arguing why Kepler’s conjecture could be verified.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAmenability versus non‐exactness of dense subgroups of a compact group - Mimura - 2019 - Journal of the London Mathematical Society - Wiley Online Library (https://londmathsoc.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1112/jlms.12229)\nciteturn12search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.5 years ago; Crawled: last month; A remarkable result of Kaluba–Nowak–Ozawa 18, property (\\mathrm{T}) for \\mathrm{Aut}({F}_{5}), together with a result of Gilman implies the following: There exists a strictly increasing sequence {({c}_{m})}_{m\\in \\mathbb{N}} of integers at least 5 such that the group {\\prod }_{m\\in \\mathbb{N}}\\mathrm{Alt}([{c}_{m}]) admits a finitely generated dense subgroup with property (\\mathrm{T}).\nRemark 6.6.A remarkable result of Kaluba–Nowak–Ozawa 18, property (\\mathrm{T}) for \\mathrm{Aut}({F}_{5}), together with a result of Gilman implies the following: There exists a strictly increasing sequence {({c}_{m})}_{m\\in \\mathbb{N}} of integers at least 5 such that the group {\\prod }_{m\\in \\mathbb{N}}\\mathrm{Alt}([{c}_{m}]) admits a finitely generated dense subgroup with property (\\mathrm{T}).\n\nWe may obtain {\\mathrm{\\Lambda }}_{4} as in the statement of Theorem B for the case where {K}_{\\mathrm{Alt}}={\\prod }_{m\\in \\mathbb{N}}\\mathrm{Alt}([{l}_{m}]), where {({l}_{m})}_{m\\in \\mathbb{N}} is a sequence of certain strictly increasing natural numbers at least 5 (but numbers of generators of {\\mathrm{\\Lambda }}_{4} may get enormous). Indeed, to construct such a {\\mathrm{\\Lambda }}_{4}, we appeal to the following result: Kassabov 19, together with Kassabov–Nikolov 21, constructed a finitely generated dense subgroup {\\mathrm{\\Lambda }}_{3} of {\\prod }_{m\\in {\\mathbb{N}}_{⩾5}}\\mathrm{Alt}([m]) such that it has property (\\tau ) (with respect to the family of all finite index subgroups); see also 20. Compare with Remark 6.4. Finally, construct {\\mathrm{\\Lambda }}_{1} and {\\mathrm{\\Lambda }}_{2} not by Lemma 6.1 but by Lemma 4.10; obtain {\\mathrm{\\Lambda }}_{1} and {\\mathrm{\\Lambda }}_{4}=⟨{\\mathrm{\\Lambda }}_{2},{\\mathrm{\\Lambda }}_{3}⟩, both dense in {K}_{\\mathrm{Alt}}, as desired.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nON THE SMALLEST NON-ABELIAN QUOTIENT OF Aut(Fₙ) (https://ora.ox.ac.uk/objects/uuid%3Adb4a48aa-6275-4682-af27-c68d5ae329af/files/srr171x459)\nciteturn12search17 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.8 years ago; BARBARA BAUMEISTER, DAWID KIELAK, AND EMILIO PIERRO ... It should be noted that such investigations, being closely related to the study of finite index subgroups of Aut(Fₙ), are connected to one of the most important open problems relating to automorphisms of free groups: for n ≥ 3, does Aut(Fₙ) or Out(Fₙ) have Kazhdan’s property (T)? These questions have negative answers for n = 3 (as shown by Grunewald–Lubotzky [GL], and positive answers for n = 5 (as shown by Kaluba–Nowak–Ozawa [KNO]).\nThe structure of finite subgroups has been understood completely thanks to the Nielsen realisation theorems for Aut(Fₙ) and Out(Fₙ) (proven independently by Culler [Cul], Khramtsov [Khr], and Zimmermann [Zim1]). In addition, there is a rich literature on both the structure of general subgroups and individual elements of Aut(Fₙ) and Out(Fₙ) – see e.g. the series of papers of Handel–Mosher [HM1, HM2, HM3, HM4, HM5] and the work of Bestvina–Feighn–Handel [BFH1, BFH2] on the train track theory.\n\nIn this article we focus on quotients of Aut(Fₙ), particularly the finite ones. It should be noted that such investigations, being closely related to the study of finite index subgroups of Aut(Fₙ), are connected to one of the most important open problems relating to automorphisms of free groups: for n ≥ 3, does Aut(Fₙ) or Out(Fₙ) have Kazhdan’s property (T)? These questions have negative answers for n = 3 (as shown by Grunewald–Lubotzky [GL], and positive answers for n = 5 (as shown by Kaluba–Nowak–Ozawa [KNO]).\n\nIt is in fact open whether either of Aut(Fₙ) or Out(Fₙ) (for n = 4 or n ≥ 6) contains a finite index subgroup which can map onto F₂, or indeed onto ℤ. (The former property is largeness, the latter is the negation of the property FAb.) Positive answers to either of these questions would lead to the negation of property (T).\n\nMore directly related to the current article is the question of whether Aut(Fₙ) satisfies the Congruence Subgroup Property – in this context, the property says that every finite quotient Aut(Fₙ) → K should factor through a (finite) quotient Aut(Fₙ) → Aut(Fₙ/χ), where χ is a finite index characteristic subgroup of Fₙ. The Congruence Subgroup Property was shown to hold for n = 2 by Asada [Asa] (see\n\nDate: January 24, 2019.\n\n1--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCheeger constant spectral clustering and eigenvalue ratios of Laplacian in Oberseminar GEOMETRIE: MPI MIS (https://www.mis.mpg.de/de/events/event/cheeger-constant-spectral-clustering-and-eigenvalue-ratios-of-laplacian)\nciteturn12search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 12.4 years ago; Crawled: 4 months ago; # Cheeger constant, spectral clustering and eigenvalue ratios of Laplacian\n\n  * Shiping Liu (University of Durham, United Kingdom) ... Its extension to compact finite-dimensional Alexandrov spaces with nonnegative curvature affirms a recent conjecture of Funano and Shioya.\n\nTalk\n\n23.06.14, 16:45\n\n# Cheeger constant, spectral clustering and eigenvalue ratios of Laplacian\n\n  * Shiping Liu (University of Durham, United Kingdom)\nA3 01 (Sophus-Lie room)\n\n* * *\n\n### Abstract\n\nIn this talk, I will explain an optimal dimension-free upper bound for eigenvalue ratios $\\lambda_k/\\lambda_1$ of the Laplacian on a closed Riemannian manifold with nonnegative Ricci curvature. This is achieved by borrowing tools from theoretical spectral clustering algorithm analysis in computer science. I will further discuss several of its applications, including improving higher-order Buser inequality, higher-order Gromov-Milman inequality and multi-way isoperimetric constant ratios estimate. Its extension to compact finite-dimensional Alexandrov spaces with nonnegative curvature affirms a recent conjecture of Funano and Shioya. On finite discrete graphs, it also has very interesting applications.\n\n* * *\n\n* * *\n\n\n07.11.96 16.05.19\n\n### Oberseminar GEOMETRIE Oberseminar GEOMETRIE\n\nMPI for Mathematics in the Sciences E1 05 (Leibniz-Saal)\n\nDetails anzeigen--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPROFUNDERE\nSCIENTIAM\nnr  15 \nstyczeń 2020\nB I U L (https://www.csz.pw.edu.pl/index.php/cont